Accent is a restrained cobalt. Use it for at most one primary action per screen.
Typography
• Headings: Newsreader or Fraunces (a serif with character), weights 500 to 600, tight tracking, large scale on the landing page.
• Body and UI: Inter Tight or DM Sans, 15 to 16 px body, 1.6 line height.
• Numbers in tables and stat tiles use tabular figures.
• Type scale: 12, 14, 16, 20, 28, 40, 64, 88 px with a maximum line length of 68 characters.
Layout and shape
• 8 px spacing grid, 12 column layout, generous whitespace, content max width 1200 px.
• Corner radius 10 px on cards and inputs, 999 px only on pills and avatars. Borders instead of heavy shadows. Shadows only on floating layers such as menus and dialogs.
• Dashboards use a left sidebar (collapsible), top bar with search and notification bell, and a content area that varies its layout by page. Mix wide charts, narrow lists, and table views rather than a grid of equal tiles.
Motion principles
• Motion explains hierarchy or state change. It is never decoration.
• Durations 150 to 250 ms for hover and press, 500 to 800 ms for scroll reveals, with ease-out cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1).
• Respect prefers-reduced-motion: replace movement with simple fades.
• Animate only transform and opacity for performance.
Hover and interaction patterns
• Cards: 2 px upward lift, border colour shifts to accent, and a small arrow slides in.
• Buttons: background deepens, 1 px press-down on active, focus ring of 2 px accent with 2 px offset.
• Links: underline grows from the left.
• Images: slow 1.04 scale on hover inside an overflow-hidden frame.
• Table rows: soft background highlight and reveal of row actions.
• Sidebar items: a vertical accent indicator slides between items using a shared layout animation.
7. Landing page specification
The landing page is the first impression and should feel like the website of a serious institution with modern craft.
Sections in order
1. Navigation bar. Logo wordmark on the left, links (Features, Roles, Security, FAQ), Login as a text link, and Sign Up as a solid button. Transparent over the hero, becomes a solid paper background with a thin bottom border after 40 px of scroll.
2. Hero. Left-aligned large serif headline, for example "Every part of college life, in one place." with a short two-line supporting paragraph, two buttons (Login, Request access), and a small line of trust text such as "Role-based access. Encrypted. Built for your campus." On the right, a real campus photograph in a tall rounded frame with a slow parallax and a floating product card overlapping its edge showing a small attendance summary. Words in the headline reveal line by line on load.
3. Department marquee. A slow, pausable horizontal strip of department names and course names, separated by thin dividers.
4. Role switcher. A tabbed section: Students, Parents, Faculty, Department Heads, Accounts, Administration. Switching a tab animates a short list of what that role gets and swaps a matching dashboard preview. This replaces a generic features grid.
5. Feature showcase (sticky scroll). A left column stays pinned with the title of the current feature while the right column scrolls through large real product screenshots built from actual components: Attendance, Results, Fees, Notices, Mentor, Leave. The active item is highlighted as you scroll.
6. By the numbers. Four large figures with count-up animation when they enter the viewport (use clearly labelled placeholder values until real ones exist).
7. How it works. Three steps (Get invited, Sign in, See your day) connected by a line that draws as you scroll.
8. Security and privacy. A calm dark section (ink background) listing real practices: role-based access, encrypted connections, audit trail, two-factor for staff, data stays within the institution. Each item has a line icon and a one-sentence explanation.
9. Notices preview. A list of three sample public notices with category tags, showing the real notice component.
10. FAQ. Accordion with smooth height animation.
11. Closing call to action. Campus photo with a dark overlay, one headline, one button.
12. Footer. Four columns (Portal, Institution, Support, Legal), address, support email, copyright, and a back-to-top control.
Landing page animation list
• Scroll progress bar at the very top of the page (2 px, accent colour).
• Staggered fade-and-rise on section headings and content blocks (24 px travel, once only).
• Hero image parallax at roughly 0.85 speed.
• Sticky scroll storytelling in section 5.
• Count-up numbers, drawing connector line, accordion height animation.
• Magnetic effect on the main call-to-action buttons (very subtle, 6 px maximum).
• Smooth anchor scrolling with a header offset.
Photography and assets
Use real photography of a campus, students, and classrooms. In AI Studio, use placeholder image URLs from a free photo source and add a note listing where to replace them with the college's own photographs. Do not use illustrated 3D characters or abstract gradient blobs.
