9. Authentication and security specification
Authentication
• Supabase Auth with email and password. Minimum 10 characters, checked against a short list of common passwords. Show a strength meter.
• Email must be verified before first login.
• Login rate limiting and temporary lockout after 5 failed attempts, with a generic error message that does not reveal whether the email exists.
• Password reset links expire in 1 hour and are single use.
• TOTP two-factor authentication required for Super Admin and Admin, optional for others.
• Inactivity logout with a warning dialog 2 minutes before expiry.
Authorisation
• Row Level Security policies on every table. The frontend hides what a role cannot use, but the database is the real gatekeeper.
• Role is read from the database profile, never from local storage, URL, or a client-editable token claim.
• Parents can read only rows for students in an approved parent_links record.
• Faculty can read and write only rows tied to their own subject assignments or mentees.
Application security
• Validate every form with Zod and re-validate on the server side (Edge Function or database constraint).
• Never render user supplied HTML. Escape output and sanitise any rich text.
• File uploads: allow only PDF, PNG, and JPG, limit to 5 MB, check type, store in private buckets, and serve through short-lived signed URLs.
• Secure HTTP headers set at the host: Content Security Policy, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, and frame protection.
• No secrets, keys, or personal data in console logs or error messages.
• Audit log entry for logins, failed logins, role changes, fee payments and reversals, attendance corrections, result publishing, and data exports.
• Export actions require re-entering the password for Admin roles.
Privacy
• Collect only the data needed. Show a clear privacy notice at sign-up.
• Students can view their own data and request correction through a ticket.
• Demo and seed data must be fictional. Never paste real student data into AI Studio or any prompt.
10. The Vibe Coding Checklist applied to this project
10.1 Master the Plan-Review-Fix cycle
1. Plan. Before any module, ask the AI to write a plan: files it will create, components, data it needs, and edge cases. Say explicitly "do not write code yet".
2. Review. Read the plan. Check it against this PRD. Correct anything wrong in plain words ("attendance must be editable for 24 hours only"). Approve with "proceed with step 1 only".
3. Fix. After each build, test it yourself: click every button, try wrong inputs, try each role. Report bugs one at a time with the exact steps, the expected result, and the actual result. Do not say "it is broken" without detail.
After every successful module, save a version in AI Studio and note it in your PROJECT_STATE file (see 10.3).
10.2 Strict security safeguards
• Never paste real student names, phone numbers, marks, or keys into prompts. Use fictional seed data.
• Keep the Supabase service role key out of the frontend, out of prompts, and out of screenshots. Only the URL and anon key go in the client.
• Ask for RLS policies before building screens that read data, and test policies with at least two accounts per role.
• Ask the AI to explain every policy in plain English so you can verify it matches Section 3.
• Run the Security Audit prompt (Prompt 13) before sharing the link with anyone.
• Turn on email verification and rate limiting in the Supabase dashboard yourself. Do not assume the generated code did it.
• Review dependencies the AI adds. Prefer well-known packages and reject anything unfamiliar.
10.3 Manage context to avoid the doom loop
The doom loop is when the AI keeps patching the same bug, each patch breaking something else. Avoid it like this:
• Keep a file in the project called PROJECT_STATE.md with: stack, folder structure, completed modules, known issues, and design tokens. Ask the AI to update it after every module.
• One module per prompt. One bug per prompt.
• If the same bug survives two attempts, stop. Revert to the last good version, then re-prompt with a more specific description or ask for a different approach.
• When the chat becomes long and the AI starts forgetting rules, open a new chat, paste Prompt 0 and the current PROJECT_STATE.md, and continue.
• Ask "before changing anything, list the files you will touch and why". This prevents rewrites of working code.
10.4 Big pictures first, then micro-details
Build in this order and do not skip ahead:
1. Structure: roles, routes, database, auth.
2. Core workflows: attendance, fees, leave, notices.
3. Remaining modules: results, mentor, timetable, tickets.
4. Visual polish: spacing, typography, motion, hover states.
5. Edge cases: empty, loading, error, offline, long text, small screens.
6. Performance, accessibility, security audit, deployment.
